WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.320
Reflect, for a moment, on just how ‘alien’ life on&nbsp;
our own planet can be. There are countless species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.520
on Earth that seem commonplace to us, but if you&nbsp;
think from the perspective of an interstellar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.240
visitor seeing them for the first time, you’ll&nbsp;
realize that much of what nature produces on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.000
daily basis seems more outlandish than the vast&nbsp;
majority of science fiction. So if we were ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.240
to discover alien life, it’s highly likely we’d&nbsp;
find it mind-blowing, and maybe even a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.520
disturbing. One science fiction project which&nbsp;
truly excels at accurately representing these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.760
likelihoods is ‘Snaiad’: which is one of the most&nbsp;
detailed speculative biology projects of all time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.720
The famous fictional world of Snaiad is the&nbsp;
creation of the one and only C. M. Kosemen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.600
author of All Tomorrows and expert in all&nbsp;
things alien, who you can follow and support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.840
in the links below. This online worldbuilding&nbsp;
project includes more than 200 extraterrestrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:04.080
lifeforms from several dozen lineages — and an&nbsp;
awe-inspiring amount of detail about each one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.960
So, for this entry into the archive, I’ll be&nbsp;
taking you on a documentary-style tour across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.160
this truly alien planet, and exploring just&nbsp;
some of the life that dwells on its surface…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.960
At first glance, the anatomy of most life&nbsp;
on Snaiad might confuse the common observer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.080
To understand the lifeforms that we’ll encounter&nbsp;
on our voyage, let’s first take a Kahydron — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.520
rather average organism by Snaiadi standards&nbsp;
— as an example. Examining the inner workings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.840
of the Kahydron, you’ll find a host of surprises.&nbsp;
Beginning with the skeletal system, Snaiadi bones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.000
aren’t made of calcium, but a modified hydrocarbon&nbsp;
composite that resembles very hard wood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.720
While massive and durable, these bones&nbsp;
burn easily, which complicates things for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.800
Snaiadi paleontologists. Snaiadi muscles are&nbsp;
equally distinctive. They work via hydraulics:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.160
moving by pushing instead of pulling — and their&nbsp;
bones have evolved to accommodate this difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.960
Strange though these muscles may be, they provide&nbsp;
their owners with incredible strength relative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.080
to their size. But the strangeness doesn’t end&nbsp;
there. Snaiadi creatures also possess a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.120
set of traditional fiber-base muscles — which&nbsp;
cover the entire body and help pump the fluid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.640
of their hydraulic counterparts. The respiratory&nbsp;
and circulatory systems of Snaiadi animals are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.760
similarly unique, with the nostrils of&nbsp;
most organisms located near their armpits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.400
These nostrils are connected to lungs that work&nbsp;
independently from one another, in a highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:36.560
distinctive set-up. Yet the most divergent system&nbsp;
in Snaiad animals is their brains. Actually two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.760
organs that work in tandem, the first component&nbsp;
of the brain is a dense knot of fibrous nerves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.560
connected to a larger nervous system, and the&nbsp;
second is a cryptic structure that seems to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:53.520
the root of consciousness each organism, although&nbsp;
the author imagines it’s poorly understood. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.640
perhaps the most noticeable visual deviation from&nbsp;
familiar life is that almost all vertebrates on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.040
Snaiads appear to have two ‘heads.’ In reality,&nbsp;
the first head contains the organism’s jaws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.480
eyes, and reproductive system, but isn’t actually&nbsp;
connected to their digestive tract at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.200
Instead, all food consumption is done via&nbsp;
the second head, which is almost like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.720
exposed throat that leads to the stomach. This&nbsp;
unconventional arrangement is a holdover from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.240
distant ancestor from which most vertebrates on&nbsp;
Snaiad are descended. In the Kahydron’s case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.760
the jaws of the first head are built for hunting,&nbsp;
as the lifeform occupies an apex predator role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.320
similar to a lion — just in its own unmistakably&nbsp;
alien fashion. Indeed, we’ll find much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.600
life on Snaiad bears some similarities to&nbsp;
an earthly species… although others truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:50.320
defy classification. A great place to continue our&nbsp;
tour is in the mountainous forests. Here, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.480
find the Urunyx, a relative of the Kahydron and&nbsp;
member of the larger Kahydroniform classification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:02.480
Like all Kahydroniformes, Urunyx is also a&nbsp;
predator, with a short, crushing bite and long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:08.320
scythe-like claws. The Urunyx is more than just&nbsp;
a true carnivore, however, but also a part-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:14.400
omnivore like various species of Earth bears. At&nbsp;
five meters, or sixteen feet in length, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.960
the powerful Urunyx rivals even the largest&nbsp;
bears in its sheer size. Within the rivers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:26.720
an equatorial jungle, you can find an aquatic&nbsp;
Kahydroniform called the Crocahydron. Roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.040
the same length as the Urunyx, the Crocahydron&nbsp;
paddles on adapted fins, snapping up organisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:40.000
with its elongated jaws. A striking mix of a leafy&nbsp;
sea dragon and an Earth crocodile, the Crocahydron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.720
is an effective predator — lying in wait before&nbsp;
snapping up its unsuspecting prey. But Snaiad is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.080
more than a world of predators. Dotting the&nbsp;
endless lush plains, the mainly-herbivorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.880
Kettleturts wander the grasslands, plucking&nbsp;
nutritious plants from the soil with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.240
second head. The Kettleturts are a member of the&nbsp;
Turtiformes, a wide-ranging group that bears some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.360
resemblance to the turtles or tortoises of our own&nbsp;
planet. Unlike their earthly analogues, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.640
the shells of Turtiformes aren’t external,&nbsp;
but are instead encased in a layer of skin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.880
Turtiformes also aren’t able to withdraw&nbsp;
their limbs into their shell when threatened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:23.360
But Turtiformes don’t just populate the land. In&nbsp;
the water, marine Turtiforms like the streamlined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.560
Cylindroides genus have become uniquely adapted&nbsp;
to their aquatic environment. Their shell has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.240
become a heavy, hydrodynamic ‘hull’ — and the&nbsp;
digits on their limbs have become broad paddles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.440
which they use to steer themselves through the&nbsp;
vast oceans much like sea turtles. On Earth, most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:44.480
species of sea turtles lay their eggs on land,&nbsp;
forcing their young to make a perilous journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.760
back to the ocean. Cleverly, however, Cylindroides&nbsp;
have evolved to birth their young without ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:55.280
leaving the water. In the forested mountain&nbsp;
ranges bordering the Great Northern Ocean, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:02.720
one-of-a-kind hunter is looking for food. This is&nbsp;
a Toxoglossos, a member of the diverse Polydactyl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:08.720
classification and a relative of the Turtiforms.&nbsp;
These tiny lifeforms possess a spring-like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:14.880
extendable second head which they use to ‘zap’&nbsp;
their unsuspecting prey. This hunting strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.240
shows a remarkable degree of convergent evolution&nbsp;
with chameleons of Earth: who use their long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:26.480
strange tongues to consume insects. Sometimes life&nbsp;
on our own planet can be downright alien as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.640
And you might find, flattened along the bottoms&nbsp;
of freshwater rivers, the peaceful Platymoloch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.040
a Polydactyl offshoot that have become&nbsp;
bottom-feeding lifeforms that spend most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.640
their time underwater. Not an active bunch--to&nbsp;
feed, the Platymoloch drag their vacuum-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.600
second heads over the muck to scoop up any&nbsp;
potential food, sort of like the feeding habits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.360
of some species of stingray: which also have&nbsp;
unusual mouths on the underside of their bodies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.800
Unlike stingrays, however, Platymoloch’s aren’t&nbsp;
particularly elegant… nor do they have much of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.560
defense against potential predators. Burrowing&nbsp;
just under the soil, the blind, unassuming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.480
Vermiphagus make their tunnels — content to be&nbsp;
ignored by the more fantastical Snaiad organisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:17.200
that stomp aboveground. Vermiphagus are our first&nbsp;
example of a Haplobrachid — a group that is united&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:23.280
by their lack of limbs, yet nonetheless have been&nbsp;
remarkably successful. Most Haplobrachids burrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:30.160
in layers of spongey vegetation called ‘sprog,’&nbsp;
which covers large swaths of Snaiad’s surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:35.920
One carnivorous Haplobrachid that tunnels within&nbsp;
the sprog are the Citognathus — meter-sized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.720
lifeforms that have evolved a distinctive&nbsp;
hunting strategy. Their second heads have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:47.280
become specialized lures that mimic the appearance&nbsp;
of common prey animals. Like the anglerfish or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:53.280
frogfish of Earth, the Citognathus simply sticks&nbsp;
the lure out and wiggles it around in a bizarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.560
manner that resembles the movement of said&nbsp;
prey, luring curious lifeforms right into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.120
predator’s jaws. A truly fascinating adaptation.&nbsp;
Impressively, some Haplobrachids have even taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.560
to the trees. Among the branches, the large,&nbsp;
sluggish Slothsnakes slither slowly towards their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:15.760
next meal of fruit or vegetation. At a surprising&nbsp;
13 feet, or 4 meters long, Slothsnakes are easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.400
the largest of the Haplobracheds group. As you&nbsp;
can imagine, their nickname comes from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.560
surface-level similarities to the slow-moving&nbsp;
sloths, and limbless arboreal snakes — although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:32.800
like many creatures on Snaiad, the slothsnakes&nbsp;
don’t neatly fit into any earthly category. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:38.560
subject of earthly (or non earthly) counterparts,&nbsp;
there are no traditional ‘insects’ on planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:45.040
Snaiad. Instead, that role is filled by curious&nbsp;
lifeforms like the 4-millimeter long Dactylopus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:50.400
These evolutionary oddities are a type of&nbsp;
Picozoan: organisms that were once larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.760
terrestrial vertebrates but became tiny&nbsp;
lifeforms over millions of years. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:02.000
organisms the size of insects require a variety of&nbsp;
adaptations to function, this grand shift in size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.600
involved various unusual shortcuts, like bones&nbsp;
that never ossify during development and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:13.840
worm-like larval stage that allows Picozoans to&nbsp;
inhabit different niches as adults and juveniles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:20.160
Look closely at the leaves in the jungles of the&nbsp;
east, and you might spot the rare Arbovermis:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:26.800
a slightly larger and more distinctive Picozoan.&nbsp;
Also called Grinchworms, these endangered green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.560
lifeforms shuffle their long, tube-like bodies&nbsp;
around on numerous tiny legs. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.440
their walking gait is likely not unlike the&nbsp;
movements of caterpillars or centipedes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:43.440
although Grinchworms don’t have quite as&nbsp;
many legs. Strange as the Picozoans are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:48.960
however, on the isolated island continent of&nbsp;
Thalassia, groups have evolved that resemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:55.040
no other lifeforms on Snaiad. For an example,&nbsp;
look no further than the carnivorous Ophictonos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:00.960
a type of Monoanticheran that lacks front limbs&nbsp;
altogether. A challenging species to decode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.240
the Ophictonos possess long, spear-like lower&nbsp;
jaws on their first heads that help them jab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:11.760
their preferred prey which dwells below ground.&nbsp;
In ‘fishing’ for organisms with their long beaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:18.160
their hunting behavior is somewhat similar to that&nbsp;
of certain birds. On the vast plains of Thalassia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:24.560
island, you can spot the herbivorous Bounderjaws,&nbsp;
a species of Monoanticherans that patrol the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:30.320
plains in vast herds. These creatures might look&nbsp;
a little off-balance, but they actually have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:36.160
highly efficient method of specialized locomotion.&nbsp;
Their legs contain piston-like hydraulic muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:42.160
that allow them to leap about, storing energy&nbsp;
from one bound to another. A bit like a kangaroo…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:48.640
although clearly only in certain regards. On&nbsp;
a nearby island, an equally atypical lifeform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:54.480
patrols the scrubland. The Red Headbanger&nbsp;
is a solitary predator with a strange name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:11:00.720
and even stranger hunting method. Charging out&nbsp;
of nowhere, the headbanger dispatches unwary prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:06.480
with hatchet-like blows of its massive head. While&nbsp;
an impressive organism, the Headbanger’s island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:12.800
isolation is key to its survival, as on a nearby&nbsp;
landmass, truly terrifying goliaths are waiting…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:19.600
Meet the Seismopus. Weighing over a ton, these&nbsp;
giants are easily one of the biggest predators on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:25.760
all of Snaiad. A type of Tromobrachid, Seismopus&nbsp;
have fused their second heads with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:31.440
‘sternums’ and front limbs — and have evolved&nbsp;
the strongest jaw-equivalents in all of Snaiad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:36.400
This gives them the advantage of being able to&nbsp;
chew and swallow from the same mouth, with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:42.880
first heads becoming thin, periscope-like organs&nbsp;
useful only for observation. Not surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:49.360
the Tromobrachids have been quite successful. Upon&nbsp;
the vast sands of the interior desert, the related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:55.920
Ammodromeus, or Sandrunner pursues prey to the&nbsp;
point of exhaustion. Like the hyenas of earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:02.320
these ruthless hunters often operate in a pack to&nbsp;
take down larger prey — although this behavior is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:07.680
likely the simple result of intersecting interests&nbsp;
rather than genuine cooperative behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:15.120
But even a pack of Sandrunners might be wary&nbsp;
of Titaniformes like Z. Urophorus, or Giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:21.360
Skipbirds — some of the mightiest herbivores&nbsp;
on Snaiad. At almost 20 feet, or six meters, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:27.200
length, Giant Skipbirds are well protected while&nbsp;
they grind down vegetation with their oversized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:32.080
plant-munching second heads. Indeed, their&nbsp;
feeding habits are somewhat like an elephant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:37.840
although Giant Skipbirds grow to even greater&nbsp;
sizes. But surprisingly, they’re not the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:44.240
of the Titaniformes… At last, we’ve reached the&nbsp;
final lifeform we’ll be archiving in this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:50.880
At almost 40 feet, or twelve meters long,&nbsp;
Legendary Magnodires are one of the largest land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:57.280
animals on Snaiad. Often simply called ‘Titans,’&nbsp;
the second head of these herbivores have become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:03.760
super-extended into long, almost drawbridge-like&nbsp;
structures. These gigantic animals spend their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:09.920
days grazing across a large circle twice as wide&nbsp;
as their second-head necks. We’ve barely covered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:15.360
half the orders in the Snaiad project, and&nbsp;
there’s many more individual species you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:22.080
read about online. In Part II, we’ll investigate&nbsp;
even larger and stranger lifeforms… but for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:27.360
our time in this incredible alien world has come&nbsp;
to an end. If you want to support the author,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:32.560
you can follow and support C.M. Kosemen using&nbsp;
the links below. He’s also working on a book that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:38.960
will overhaul many of the species with updated&nbsp;
details, which I’m really excited for… Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.000
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:14:01.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

